2.0.1 (unreleased)
  - Security: the bundled MaxMind DB reader is updated from 1.13.0 to 1.14.0
    (limits for the decoding of malformed or hostile database files). Its
    version is now tracked in composer.json/composer.lock; src/Helper/Geo is
    generated from it (tools/vendor-geoip.php), CI verifies that, and
    Dependabot plus a weekly job report new releases and advisories
  - Blocking through .htaccess: blocks that ran out or were lifted are now
    removed from the file (before, a timed block stayed there for ever and
    the file grew with every blocked address); at most 2000 lines are kept;
    the file is replaced atomically instead of being rewritten in place; the
    lock file moved from the system's temp directory to Joomla's
  - Failed logins are purged after 4 weeks by default (set the purge age to 0
    for the old behaviour) and limited to 200000 rows
  - A response is delayed by 60 seconds at most, however the delay settings
    add up, and the account throttle no longer delays a blocked address
  - Unblock tokens are stored as hashes; links sent before the update stop
    working (they are valid for 3 days only)
  - Usernames in log entries and in mails can no longer contain line breaks
  - Array-valued request parameters ("option[]=x") no longer cause a
    TypeError in the check of blocked requests
  - Settings which have not been saved yet now have the values the settings
    page shows (block after 10 attempts instead of 15, ...)
  - Component: all list settings are validated against their options, the
    notification addresses against the email format; messages escape what was
    typed in; settings and log need the core.admin permission

2.0.0 (2026-08-24)
  - Migrate to Joomla 5/6: PSR-4 namespaced classes (Codeling\Plugin\System\Bfstop),
    service provider, SubscriberInterface event handling
  - Drop Joomla 3/4 compatibility (requires Joomla 5 or 6)
  - Modernize token generation to use random_bytes()/random_int()
  - Consolidate all configuration into the component's Settings view
    (Components -> Brute Force Stop -> Settings); the plugin's Options tab
    and description now just point there instead of holding real settings
  - Adaptive allowance of failed attempts (#76): failed logins for a
    username are now also throttled across all source IPs combined, not
    just per IP, so a distributed attack spreading attempts against one
    account across many IPs can no longer evade the per-IP threshold; a
    new per-attempt risk score (known IP/username pairs, common usernames,
    missing User-Agent, optional GeoIP country and reverse-DNS signals)
    adjusts the delay and allowed-attempts threshold; optional progressive
    (exponential) block duration for repeat offenders; password reset/
    username reminder now stay reachable even for a blocked IP
  - Local GeoIP lookup (issue #169): "Information for IP Address" in the
    component now shows real country/region/city/coordinates again via a
    locally-configured MaxMind .mmdb database, instead of the discontinued
    freegeoip.net API it used to call
  - Username statistics (#136): the number of failed logins per username,
    with first and last attempt, is now kept in a separate table which is
    not affected by the automatic purge of old failed login entries; it is
    seeded from the retained failed login entries on update, and shown as
    a "highscore" of attacked usernames in the component
  - All BFStop tables now use the utf8mb4 character set (like Joomla's own
    tables), so failed logins for usernames containing 4-byte characters
    (e.g. emoji) are recorded too, instead of failing to be stored
  - Manual cleanup of failed login entries (#214): the component's failed
    login list has a "Delete Old Entries" button, which asks for the age (in
    days) of the entries to keep and deletes all older ones (blocks are not
    affected), and a hint pointing at the setting for automatically deleting
    old entries
  - Not implemented (documented as future work): CAPTCHA/challenge-based
    responses and IP-reputation/hosting-vs-residential classification were
    both considered but require either login-page changes or an
    additional external database dependency; a real risk-based
    step-up-authentication flow (e.g. requiring Joomla's built-in
    two-factor auth when risk is high) would need to hook earlier in the
    login pipeline than this plugin currently does and is left for a
    separate, larger initiative
  - PostgreSQL support (#206): install/uninstall scripts for PostgreSQL;
    the plugin previously installed without creating its tables there and
    then silently never blocked anything. IP subnet matching for block and
    allow list entries is now done in PHP instead of with MySQL-only SQL
    functions, and fixed-length time windows are computed in PHP
  - Security: an emailed unblock token no longer lets a blocked IP address
    bypass the block. It was accepted on any request with
    view=tokenunblock (e.g. a login), was never used up by such a request
    and was not checked for expiry, so anyone who got a token (e.g. by
    getting their own account blocked) could keep guessing passwords from
    a blocked IP. Now it only passes the unblock page of com_bfstop itself,
    only while unexpired, and only if issued for a block of the requesting
    IP address; the token is no longer written to the log either
  - Security: behind a proxy/load balancer, the client IP is now taken from
    the end of the forwarding header (the entry added by the trusted proxy)
    instead of its first public entry, which a visitor could forge to evade
    blocking, get other addresses blocked or impersonate an allowlisted one.
    Several trusted proxies (and CIDR subnets) can be configured, the
    "Forwarded" header (RFC 7239) and addresses with ports are understood,
    and private client addresses reported by the proxy are accepted. Not
    all proxies append to an incoming header: pick a header yours sets or
    appends to
  - Security: a failed login is now recorded, counted and checked against
    the block threshold before the configured delay is applied, not after
    it, and a request whose IP address just got (or already is) blocked is
    not delayed any more. Before, a burst of parallel login attempts all
    ran before the first of them was counted, and every one of them held
    on to a server worker for the whole delay
  - Security: usernames of failed logins which do not belong to an existing
    account (and are not on the list of common usernames) are now stored,
    shown, logged and mailed as an unreadable hash by default, as users
    often type their password into the username field; the hash is the same
    for the same input, so attempts are still counted together. The new
    setting "Usernames Not Matching an Account" restores the old behaviour.
    Entries stored by earlier versions stay as they are - purge them in the
    failed login and username statistics views if you want them gone
  - Security: unblocking by the link in the notification email now needs a
    confirmation (a POST request) and only works from the IP address which
    was blocked, so mail security scanners or link previews which open
    every link in a message can't unblock an attacker (see com_bfstop)
  - Security: the "User Block Message" with the unblock link is no longer
    sent if the failed logins from the blocked IP address which led to the
    block also went against other existing accounts. The link goes to the
    owner of the account of the last attempt, so an attacker with an account
    of their own could otherwise end every series of guesses at other
    accounts with an attempt at their own, get the link and continue.
    Attempts against names which don't exist (e.g. typos) don't count
  - Security: anybody can make the plugin send the "User Block Message" to
    a user, by failing to log in with that username from an address until it
    is blocked - a way to flood somebody's inbox. The setting now has three
    values: disabled; enabled only if the user has logged in from the
    blocked IP address (or, for IPv6, its network) before, which is the
    case for somebody locking themselves out but never for an attacker's
    address; and enabled for any IP address, where a user gets at most one
    unblock link at a time which can still be used (not yet expired or used
    up). Note that the previous "enabled" now means the first of these; the
    old behaviour is the third. Known IP addresses are recorded at
    successful logins, so users get the message again after having logged
    in once. The username a link was sent to is recorded with its token
  - The addresses users logged in from (used for the risk score and for the
    unblock message) are remembered by network for IPv6 clients, as failed
    logins are, so a client whose address changes within its /64 every day
    stays a known one and doesn't add a row per address; the lookup ignores
    the case of the username. They are forgotten a year after the last login
    from them (and the table is limited to 100000 rows, oldest first). This
    maintenance, like the limit of the username statistics, runs once a day
    whatever the purge age is set to
  - Unblock tokens which have expired are deleted by the daily maintenance too
    (before: only when somebody visited the unblock page, or by the purge by
    age if one is configured). The status code of the block page is set with
    http_response_code() instead of a raw "HTTP/1.0 403" header, which hard-coded
    the protocol version
  - The unblock page of com_bfstop answers with an error status if the link
    can't be used (400 without a token, 403 from another IP address, 404 for
    an unknown, expired or used token, 500 if unblocking failed), unless "Use
    HTTP Error" is switched off; opening the link (a GET request) stays a 200
    for every token. The page is not cached and tells browsers not to send
    its address (which contains the token) to other sites and search engines
    not to index it
  - Security: IPv6 clients are now tracked per network (default: /64, see
    the new "IPv6 Tracking Granularity" setting) instead of per address:
    failed logins are counted and blocks apply to the whole network, so an
    attacker can no longer start over by switching to another address in
    their /64. IPv4-mapped, NAT64, 6to4 and Teredo addresses, which embed
    unrelated clients' IPv4 addresses, are still tracked individually
  - Security: the block page is never cached (Cache-Control: no-store) and,
    if no setting says otherwise, answers with the HTTP status 403 instead of
    200, so a caching proxy/CDN can't serve it to other visitors
  - Security: only a valid IP address or subnet is written to the .htaccess
    file (before, a value like "192.0.2.0/1e1" passed the backend validation
    and made Apache reject the whole file); the 403 message is escaped; and
    changes of the file are done while holding a lock, so that clients
    getting blocked at the same time don't overwrite each other's lines
  - Log file (#185): besides being rotated once it exceeds a configurable size
    (default 5 MB), entries older than a configurable number of days (default
    28) are now deleted from it once a day, also when logging was disabled in
    the meantime; the log files are removed when the plugin is uninstalled, and
    the component's log view got buttons to prune or clear the log
  - Security: the plugin's log file is rotated at 5 MB, and the username
    statistics (which aren't purged by age) are limited to 10000 usernames,
    removing those with the fewest attempts first, so a visitor can't fill up
    disk or database by making up usernames
  - Security: the periodic maintenance (once a day) only updates its own
    timestamp in the plugin's stored settings, instead of writing back all
    settings as they were when the request started, which could undo a
    change an administrator saved in the meantime; and it now also runs if
    no purge age is configured
  - Security (component): sending the test email now needs the permission to
    change the settings; the .htaccess path and GeoIP database path settings
    are validated (no stream wrappers like phar://, a .mmdb file name)
  - Added SECURITY.md with the supported versions and how to report
    vulnerabilities

